<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of London (1915)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as London Pact · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of London was a secret agreement signed on 26 April 1915 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Italy left the Triple Alliance and promised to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on the Entente side within a month, in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, Istria, part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and other gains. Its partial fulfilment at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> fed the Italian grievance of a 'mutilated victory'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915.jpg" alt="Treaty of London (1915)" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Smol2204 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territories_promised_to_Italy_in_the_treaty_of_London_(1915).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Soon after the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-entente">Triple Entente</a> powers, the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, sought more allies. The first attempt to bring in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> came in August and September 1914, on Russian initiative. On 4 August, a day after Italy declared neutrality, its ambassador to Russia said Italy might join the Entente in return for Trentino-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> and a dominant position in the Adriatic. The British Foreign Secretary, Edward Grey, would consider nothing further until Italy committed itself, and the Italian prime minister, Antonio Salandra, said that was impossible at the time.</p><p>Talks were taken up again by Salandra's foreign minister Sidney Sonnino, who on 4 March 1915 presented Grey with sixteen conditions. The Entente was at the same time courting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and the Italian claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> clashed with the Adriatic outlet offered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> as compensation. Grey judged the Italian claims excessive but not against British interests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s foreign minister, Sergey Sazonov, objected to Italian claims in Dalmatia, and negotiations ran on for six weeks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Article 1 provided for a military agreement guaranteeing the number of troops <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> would commit against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. Article 2 required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to enter the war against all the enemies of the three Entente powers, and the French and British navies were obliged by Article 3 to support it by destroying the Austro-Hungarian fleet. Under Article 4 Italy received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, with a new frontier between the Piz Umbrail and Toblach and an eastern frontier from Tarvisio to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvarner-gulf">Kvarner Gulf</a> that left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a> outside Italy.</p><p>Article 5 awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> north of a line running northeast from Cape Planka, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> and Šibenik, and all the Austro-Hungarian Adriatic islands except a list of twelve. The remaining coast between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a> and the Drin River went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Although it was meant to be secret, an outline became known in late April 1915 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a>, which with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> protested strongly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in May 1915 but waited a year before declaring war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom resented. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> pressed to void the treaty as contrary to self-determination. Italy received Trentino-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a> and the Julian March, and under the Treaty of Rapallo Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, but the Entente did not provide the promised colonies or part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>The result turned wartime fervour into nationalist resentment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a> called the outcome a mutilated victory and led veterans and soldiers to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Rijeka</a> in the Impresa di Fiume. His short-lived Italian Regency of Carnaro influenced the development of Italian fascism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of London (1915)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abandoning the Triple Alliance with Germany and Austria-Hungary, Italy joined the Entente under the <b>Treaty of London</b> of April 1915 and entered the First World War that May.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…damaged relations between Austria-Hungary and Russia and Serbia and was among the causes of World War I. The <b>Treaty of London</b> of May 1913 recognised Kosovo and north-western Macedonia as Serbian, and the Treaty of Bucharest of 1913 gave it Vardar Macedonia, though the new Principality of Albania…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
